## Why it is vendored

The driver has no mechanism to register a custom authentication plugin. Its
auth handshake is a fixed switch over built-in plugin names. StarRocks
authenticates a JWT user through a custom client plugin,
`authentication_openid_connect_client`, which the upstream driver does not
implement. Adding it requires editing the driver source, so a minimal in-tree
fork is the only option. The project points at exactly one MySQL driver; this
copy replaces the upstream dependency for the StarRocks client.

## Modifications by KubedAI (semantic-operator)

Under MPL 2.0, modified files remain under MPL 2.0 and their source is
available here. The changes are:

1. driver.go — the driver registers with database/sql under the name
   `starrocks-mysql` instead of `mysql`, so this fork never collides with an
   upstream driver that may also be linked.
2. auth.go — added the `authentication_openid_connect_client` case to the
   `auth()` switch. It emits one capability byte followed by the
   length-encoded JWT, which is the format
   StarRocks `JWTAuthenticationProvider` reads. The token is carried in the
   DSN password field.
3. auth_openid_test.go — added tests that assert the plugin wire format, to
   guard the change across future rebases.

Each modified source file keeps its original MPL 2.0 header and carries an
inline "MODIFIED by KubedAI" marker at the change site.

## Maintenance

This copy is pinned at v1.10.0. Rebase only when an upstream fix is needed.
To rebase: replace the tree with the new upstream tag, reapply the three
changes above, and run the tests. The durable fix is to upstream the plugin
to go-sql-driver/mysql and retire this fork.
